WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.660
API input arrives through route values, query strings, headers, cookies, JSON bodies, multipart content, files, and configuration.

2
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:16.280
A value can parse successfully and still be wrong for the current operation.

3
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:24.060
It may refer to the wrong tenant, exceed a business limit, request a disallowed state transition, or carry a field the caller should never control.

4
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.680
Treat API input as untrusted until the server validates it for the exact decision being made.

5
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.880
DTOs are boundary objects.

6
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:40.460
They should describe what this API operation accepts or returns, not simply mirror database entities or internal domain objects.

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.920
A request DTO for creating a record may need different fields from an update DTO, an administrative DTO, or a response DTO.

8
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:55.060
Intentional shape reduces over-posting, accidental field exposure, and confusion about which properties are caller-controlled.

9
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.660
Model binding is object population.

10
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:05.360
It helps map request data into .NET objects, but it does not prove the data is safe, complete, authorized, or appropriate.

11
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:19.140
If sensitive fields such as ownership, price, role, approval status, tenant ID, or workflow state appear in a bindable input model, a caller may be able to send values the server never meant to accept.

12
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.960
Operation-specific DTOs are often safer than broad shared models.

13
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.520
Server-side validation is the enforcement point.

14
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:38.180
Client-side checks, generated clients, OpenAPI schemas, and mobile app validation improve usability, but the API must validate the rules it relies on.

15
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:47.080
Use allow-lists for known fields, formats, ranges, states, identifiers, sort options, and content types.

16
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:54.220
Validate both syntax and meaning, because a well-formed value can still be inappropriate for the current resource or user.

17
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.020
Normalize before security-relevant comparisons.

18
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:10.920
Case, whitespace, URL encoding, Unicode representation, culture-specific formatting, path representation, and alternate identifiers can affect equality and authorization decisions.

19
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:16.540
Convert inputs to the representation the API trusts, then compare or enforce policy.

20
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:24.880
This is especially important for identifiers, file labels, tenant keys, email-like values, route segments, and state names.

21
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:31.320
Validation errors should help legitimate callers fix problems without exposing internals.

22
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:39.660
A good API error can identify the invalid field, explain a safe correction, and remain consistent across clients.

23
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:51.680
It should not reveal database details, internal enum values that are not part of the contract, sensitive record existence, stack traces, service names, or rules that create unnecessary operational exposure.

24
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.900
Clear and bounded errors make APIs easier to use and safer to operate.
